-authored 'pattern table' and 'Detection is tuned by measurement against the live store rather than by argument.'","No mention anywhere in the plan of gitleaks, detect-secrets, trufflehog, or GitHub secret-scanning pattern sets, nor a rationale for a bespoke table over consuming a maintained one.","Web search (retrieved): gitleaks is 'a rule-first scanner ... regex patterns, with Shannon entropy'; Yelp detect-secrets is 'a plugin-based Python scanner' for detecting secrets — both maintain credential-family regex tables as their primary value.","Plan's own drift evidence: lint.py 'had already drifted — it lacked the Anthropic/OpenAI/Atlassian/PyPI families the incident actually carried', which is exactly the table-staleness failure mode a maintained external pattern set addresses."],"finding":"The plan builds a bespoke in-repo secret-detection engine (regex family table + fixed-width entropy window + line-anchored PEM in src/rebar/secret_screen.py) without considering established prior art for the detection layer specifically — e.g. gitleaks (regex + Shannon-entropy rule engine) or Yelp detect-secrets (plugin-based regex/entropy pattern set, pip-installable), both of which maintain and update credential-family tables as a core deliverable.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f8dead5ad872a219d","impact":0.0,"is_primary":null,"location":"Approach section / Scope (new src/rebar/secret_screen.py pattern table)","norm_id":"n00574bb24391b9b2","priority":0.0,"reason":"default-advisory","scenarios":["A new provider key format (or a rotated Anthropic key prefix) appears after ship; the hand-maintained rebar table lags, and a genuine credential slips through the screen again — the same drift that left lint.py incomplete.","Reviewers cannot tell whether the bespoke families are as complete/current as an off-the-shelf table because no comparison was made."],"severity":"none","suggested_fix":"Add a short prior-art note to the Approach: name the established tools (gitleaks / detect-secrets), state the constraints that rule out taking a runtime dependency (deterministic pure function on the synchronous write path, no network, no baseline file), and confirm the family table was cross-checked against one of those tools' rule sets so the in-repo table is not merely re-derived and prone to the same drift seen in lint.py.","tier":"LLM","validity":0.625,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"yes","asserted_capability_confirmed":"na","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"yes","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"yes","impact_follows_necessarily":"no","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"yes","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"no","path_reachable":"yes","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"yes","severity_claim_justified":"no"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"none","blast_radius":"module","debt_impact":"medium","divergent_implementation":"none","dod_uncertifiable":"none","internal_conflict":"none","irreversible_without_rationale":"none","likelihood":"medium","prod_impact":"low","reversibility":"moderate","silent_vs_self_revealing":"silent","undecomposed":"none","vague_directive":"none"}}},{"block_threshold":0.8,"blocking_enabled":true,"checklist_item":"","cohort":null,"criteria":["E4"],"decision":"dropped","drop_reason":null,"evidence":[],"finding":"The plan's root cause #2, fix step 2, and AC3 assert the push path swallows the git rejection reason (GH013 line + offending path), but the store push module already logs it. push.py:233-243 detects the non-retriable class (a policy decline such as GH013), and logs 'tickets branch push failed (exit %s): %s%s' with the full git stderr (which contains the GH013 violation line + path) plus the unpushed backlog summary — with an inline comment crediting 'bug 2a76. Report the reason git gave AND the backlog size.' Even the retries-exhausted branch (push.py:366-370) now emits stder